00:24:16,130 --> 00:24:18,370
the executable file also belongs to,

566
00:24:18,370 --> 00:24:20,250
it uses the ELF file format.

567
00:24:20,250 --> 00:24:23,250
And then there are .so files,

568
00:24:23,250 --> 00:24:25,770
which are what we call shared library files.

569
00:24:25,770 --> 00:24:27,470
This file,

570
00:24:27,470 --> 00:24:29,590
it can, at run time,

571
00:24:29,590 --> 00:24:31,030
be dynamically linked,

572
00:24:31,030 --> 00:24:32,790
unlike before, where we generally,

573
00:24:32,790 --> 00:24:34,710
the most common is static linking.

574
00:24:34,710 --> 00:24:36,110
It can, at run time,

575
00:24:36,110 --> 00:24:38,750
be linked with executable files.

576
00:24:38,750 --> 00:24:41,290
And these shared library files,

577
00:24:41,290 --> 00:24:43,530
also use the ELF file format.

578
00:24:43,530 --> 00:24:46,070
And the last type,

579
00:24:46,070 --> 00:24:47,150
is the .ko file.

580
00:24:47,150 --> 00:24:50,190
I don't know if any of you have come across this file.

581
00:24:50,190 --> 00:24:51,630
That is to say, when

582
00:24:51,630 --> 00:24:54,050
for example, a program crashes,

583
00:24:54,050 --> 00:24:55,470
it might

584
00:24:55,470 --> 00:24:58,090
Of course, this requires us to configure it in the system.

585
00:24:58,090 --> 00:24:59,430
By default, it's not generated.

586
00:24:59,430 --> 00:25:01,630
But once you've configured it,

587
00:25:01,630 --> 00:25:03,810
you can specify that when a program crashes,

588
00:25:03,810 --> 00:25:06,050
it can generate a core dump file.

589
00:25:06,050 --> 00:25:07,850
Now, inside this core dump file,

590
00:25:07,850 --> 00:25:10,290
it contains a lot of information from the time the program crashed—

591
00:25:10,290 --> 00:25:12,790
on-site environment details,

592
00:25:12,790 --> 00:25:16,230
which can help us figure out what exactly

593
00:25:16,230 --> 00:25:18,770
happened on the system when the program crashed.

594
00:25:18,770 --> 00:25:20,730
Now, this kind of core dump file

595
00:25:20,730 --> 00:25:22,910
uses the ELF file format.

596
00:25:22,910 --> 00:25:25,070
So that's why this ELF file format

597
00:25:25,070 --> 00:25:25,630
is extremely important.

598
00:25:25,630 --> 00:25:26,610
If you are

599
00:25:26,610 --> 00:25:29,030
optimizing programs,

600
00:25:29,030 --> 00:25:30,470
debugging and troubleshooting them,

601
00:25:30,470 --> 00:25:34,030
Some of the more complicated problems,

602
00:25:34,030 --> 00:25:35,070
they may involve

603
00:25:35,070 --> 00:25:36,990
okay,

604
00:25:36,990 --> 00:25:37,270
this,

605
00:25:37,270 --> 00:25:39,870
this basic concept.

606
00:25:39,870 --> 00:25:43,550
So what does this ERF file format actually look like?

607
00:25:43,550 --> 00:25:44,450
We've drawn a diagram here

608
00:25:44,450 --> 00:25:46,310
for everyone.

609
00:25:46,310 --> 00:25:47,230
You can take a look at it.

610
00:25:47,230 --> 00:25:50,330
This ERF file,

611
00:25:50,330 --> 00:25:51,090
first of all,

612
00:25:51,090 --> 00:25:51,370
it

613
00:25:51,370 --> 00:25:53,290
if you just use VI

614
00:25:53,290 --> 00:25:54,970
or a text editor to open it,

615
00:25:54,970 --> 00:25:56,110
you won't be able to read it clearly,

616
00:25:56,110 --> 00:25:58,350
because it's a binary format.

617
00:25:58,350 --> 00:26:00,470
But it actually does have a structure.

618
00:26:00,470 --> 00:26:02,810
The first part of it is called the file header.

619
00:26:02,810 --> 00:26:04,670
Inside this file header,

620
00:26:04,670 --> 00:26:07,750
it contains some basic information about the ERF file.

621
00:26:07,750 --> 00:26:09,650
For example, this program —

622
00:26:09,650 --> 00:26:12,470
which architecture is this file meant to run on?

623
00:26:12,470 --> 00:26:13,410
Because as we know,

624
00:26:13,410 --> 00:26:14,690
with different architectures,

625
00:26:14,690 --> 00:26:16,290
the information inside this ELF,

626
00:26:16,290 --> 00:26:17,330
it could be different.

627
00:26:17,330 --> 00:26:18,650
So it needs to, in this header,

628
00:26:18,650 --> 00:26:20,230
interpret that information.

629
00:26:20,230 --> 00:26:23,030
This is the header.

630
00:26:23,030 --> 00:26:24,790
And then there's some version number information,

631
00:26:24,790 --> 00:26:25,490
all in this place.

632
00:26:25,490 --> 00:26:29,230
Now, right after the ELF file header,

633
00:26:29,230 --> 00:26:29,970
in the part below it,

634
00:26:29,970 --> 00:26:30,910
there's something called

635
00:26:30,910 --> 00:26:33,010
the Program Head Tail.

636
00:26:33,010 --> 00:26:34,890
We'll talk about this later.

637
00:26:34,890 --> 00:26:37,650
Let's first look at the main body in the middle,

638
00:26:37,650 --> 00:26:38,510
which is the white part.

639
00:26:38,510 --> 00:26:39,670
This part,

640
00:26:39,670 --> 00:26:41,110
it's a series of one after another...

641
00:26:41,110 --> 00:26:42,650
We call it

642
00:26:42,650 --> 00:26:43,990
a section

643
00:26:43,990 --> 00:26:46,730
for example, this one of ours,

644
00:26:46,730 --> 00:26:47,890
what's placed inside it is

645
00:26:47,890 --> 00:26:49,250
different types of content.

646
00:26:49,250 --> 00:26:50,510
For example, .txt,

647
00:26:50,510 --> 00:26:51,490
inside this one,

648
00:26:51,490 --> 00:26:52,410
it holds a bunch of

649
00:26:52,410 --> 00:26:54,250
program instructions —

650
00:26:54,250 --> 00:26:56,430
that is, the executable program

651
00:26:56,430 --> 00:26:57,310
is placed right here.

652
00:26:57,310 --> 00:26:58,970
init, well,

653
00:26:58,970 --> 00:26:59,970
is probably used for

654
00:26:59,970 --> 00:27:01,410
some initialization instructions.

655
00:27:01,410 --> 00:27:02,530
data, well,

656
00:27:02,530 --> 00:27:03,830
probably holds some

657
00:27:03,830 --> 00:27:05,490
data —

658
00:27:05,490 --> 00:27:07,970
for instance, some of the things we

659
00:27:07,970 --> 00:27:08,690
define in the program,

660
00:27:08,690 --> 00:27:09,610
global variables.

661
00:27:09,610 --> 00:27:11,410
It's placed right here.

662
00:27:11,410 --> 00:27:12,530
These concepts.

663
00:27:12,530 --> 00:27:13,730
I don't know if everyone...

664
00:27:13,730 --> 00:27:15,070
Whether there was a course earlier...

665
00:27:15,070 --> 00:27:15,790
Whether this was covered...

666
00:27:15,790 --> 00:27:17,190
Whether this was covered before...

667
00:27:17,190 --> 00:27:18,230
This part of the content.

668
00:27:18,230 --> 00:27:21,110
It was covered, right?

669
00:27:21,110 --> 00:27:22,230
If you've covered it...

670
00:27:22,230 --> 00:27:23,310
It'll probably sound a bit easier to follow.

671
00:27:23,310 --> 00:27:24,090
For us here...

672
00:27:24,090 --> 00:27:25,210
Since we're going through it quite fast...

673
00:27:25,210 --> 00:27:27,980
So this...

674
00:27:27,980 --> 00:27:30,460
So in this file of ours...

675
00:27:30,460 --> 00:27:32,120
What sections are actually in it?

676
00:27:32,120 --> 00:27:33,240
It's actually through...

677
00:27:33,240 --> 00:27:34,780
The blue part at the end here...

678
00:27:34,780 --> 00:27:36,480
which is called the section head table...

679
00:27:36,480 --> 00:27:37,220
that it's described.

680
00:27:37,220 --> 00:27:38,880
As for my blue line here...

681
00:27:38,880 --> 00:27:40,060
It basically expresses the idea that

682
00:27:40,060 --> 00:27:41,840
if we look at

683
00:27:41,840 --> 00:27:43,400
the section header table,

684
00:27:43,400 --> 00:27:44,640
we can find out

685
00:27:44,640 --> 00:27:45,800
what sections exist

686
00:27:45,800 --> 00:27:47,720
in this file,

687
00:27:47,720 --> 00:27:49,220
what each of these sections

688
00:27:49,220 --> 00:27:52,660
means,

689
00:27:52,660 --> 00:27:54,300
how big each of them is,

690
00:27:54,300 --> 00:27:55,060
and where it is located.

691
00:27:55,060 --> 00:27:58,760
All of this is described in the section header table.

692
00:27:58,760 --> 00:28:01,520
So, we can think of it this way:

693
00:28:01,520 --> 00:28:03,600
this section header table,

694
00:28:03,600 --> 00:28:06,240
this part,

695
00:28:06,240 --> 00:28:07,520
actually describes,

696
00:28:07,520 --> 00:28:11,620
describes the contents of this file from a linking perspective,

697
00:28:11,620 --> 00:28:16,080
because linking involves this part of the content.

698
00:28:16,080 --> 00:28:19,900
Now, these parts,

699
00:28:19,900 --> 00:28:21,820
we call them sections one by one,

700
00:28:21,820 --> 00:28:22,960
which is the concept of a section.

701
00:28:22,960 --> 00:28:24,240
In Chinese, we call it a section.

702
00:28:24,240 --> 00:28:26,340
OK, so now let's come back to

703
00:28:26,340 --> 00:28:27,180
what we were just talking about,

704
00:28:27,180 --> 00:28:28,760
this red part at the front,

705
00:28:28,760 --> 00:28:30,880
which is the program header table.

706
00:28:30,880 --> 00:28:33,080
What information is it actually used to hold?

707
00:28:33,080 --> 00:28:35,960
And why do we need to introduce

708
00:28:35,960 --> 00:28:37,560
this program header table?

709
00:28:37,560 --> 00:28:39,140
Well, it's actually a kind of...

710
00:28:39,140 --> 00:28:40,680
what we call...

711
00:28:40,680 --> 00:28:42,020
it actually describes a

712
00:28:42,020 --> 00:28:43,560
concept called the run-time view.

713
00:28:43,560 --> 00:28:46,440
So what is the run-time view?

714
00:28:46,440 --> 00:28:47,760
It means that when we...

715
00:28:47,760 --> 00:28:49,280
the file we ultimately generate,

716
00:28:49,280 --> 00:28:50,240
we know that this file,

717
00:28:50,240 --> 00:28:51,920
let's say it's an executable file,

718
00:28:51,920 --> 00:28:53,060
then this file,

719
00:28:53,060 --> 00:28:54,880
when we finally run it,

720
00:28:54,880 --> 00:28:56,680
it has to be loaded somewhere first, right?

721
00:28:56,680 --> 00:28:58,300
loaded into memory

722
00:28:58,300 --> 00:28:58,840
Think about it, isn't that right?

723
00:28:58,840 --> 00:29:01,160
So during loading,

724
00:29:01,160 --> 00:29:02,260
these sections,

725
00:29:02,260 --> 00:29:04,480
if we don't distinguish between them

726
00:29:04,480 --> 00:29:05,420
and just load them one by one,

727
00:29:05,420 --> 00:29:07,540
that is, load each of them in separately,

728
00:29:07,540 --> 00:29:09,140
that could introduce a problem.

729
00:29:09,140 --> 00:29:09,620
What problem?

730
00:29:09,620 --> 00:29:11,520
Well, as we know, our operating system,

731
00:29:11,520 --> 00:29:13,960
when loading these sections of ours,

732
00:29:13,960 --> 00:29:14,860
in general,

733
00:29:14,860 --> 00:29:17,680
will align them to a certain width.

734
00:29:17,680 --> 00:29:20,100
What does width alignment mean?

735
00:29:20,100 --> 00:29:22,920
For example, if it aligns them at 4K,

736
00:29:22,920 --> 00:29:24,620
then

737
00:29:24,620 --> 00:29:25,340
that means,

738
00:29:25,340 --> 00:29:27,960
say this .txt section,

739
00:29:27,960 --> 00:29:30,520
say it takes up 40 bytes,

740
00:29:30,520 --> 00:29:35,020
it will still occupy a 4K-sized region.

741
00:29:35,020 --> 00:29:36,020
for the rest of it,

742
00:29:36,020 --> 00:29:38,000
it won't put anything else in there.

743
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:40,220
Then the next section, say init,

744
00:29:40,220 --> 00:29:43,720
it might have to start being laid out from the next 4K byte position.

745
00:29:43,720 --> 00:29:44,820
So think about it.

746
00:29:44,820 --> 00:29:45,840
So, you see,

747
00:29:45,840 --> 00:29:46,420
in that case,

748
00:29:46,420 --> 00:29:48,020
if we always do it this way,

749
00:29:48,020 --> 00:29:51,100
then when these are put into memory,

750
00:29:51,100 --> 00:29:52,540
even though my file is not big,

751
00:29:52,540 --> 00:29:54,060
once it gets unpacked into memory,

752
00:29:54,060 --> 00:29:55,880
it might take up a lot of memory.

753
00:29:55,880 --> 00:29:56,780
That's quite a waste.

754
00:29:56,780 --> 00:29:58,460
Do you all get what I mean?

755
00:29:58,460 --> 00:29:59,900
So what do we do?

756
00:29:59,900 --> 00:30:02,800
Well, when we actually load it into

757
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:04,880
memory to run it,

758
00:30:04,880 --> 00:30:07,080
we actually take these sections

759
00:30:07,080 --> 00:30:08,620
and merge them together,

760
00:30:08,620 --> 00:30:10,460
and when we merge them,

761
00:30:10,460 --> 00:30:13,900
these sections are merged based on their attributes

762
00:30:13,900 --> 00:30:15,320
For example, we will see that

763
00:30:15,320 --> 00:30:19,220
in both the text section and the init section,

764
00:30:19,220 --> 00:30:21,300
what's stored is executable program instructions.

765
00:30:21,300 --> 00:30:22,980
So we can put them together.

766
00:30:22,980 --> 00:30:23,580
Think about it, doesn't that make sense?

767
00:30:23,580 --> 00:30:25,660
So by putting them together,

768
00:30:25,660 --> 00:30:27,300
we merge them.

769
00:30:27,300 --> 00:30:29,320
This curly brace means they are merged together.

770
00:30:29,320 --> 00:30:30,260
After merging them,

771
00:30:30,260 --> 00:30:32,040
we call the result a

772
00:30:32,040 --> 00:30:33,540
segment—that is, a segment.

773
00:30:33,540 --> 00:30:37,360
When we actually load it into memory,

774
00:30:37,360 --> 00:30:39,820
the loading is done in units of segments.

775
00:30:39,820 --> 00:30:43,000
Each segment may contain one or more sections.

776
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:45,100
The main purpose,

777
00:30:45,100 --> 00:30:46,760
as I just mentioned, is to save memory.

778
00:30:46,760 --> 00:30:49,800
Otherwise, the wasted space in between would be far too large.

779
00:30:49,800 --> 00:30:51,640
So, that's why we

780
00:30:51,640 --> 00:30:53,440
take this pro

781
00:30:53,440 --> 00:30:56,140
Which of these sections go together

782
00:30:56,140 --> 00:30:59,640
how big each of these grouped segments should be

783
00:30:59,640 --> 00:31:00,740
how much space they take up

784
00:31:00,740 --> 00:31:02,700
and exactly where they get loaded

785
00:31:02,700 --> 00:31:04,100
The description of all this information

786
00:31:04,100 --> 00:31:07,260
is all specified in the program head table

787
00:31:07,260 --> 00:31:09,080
Now, in this table,

788
00:31:09,080 --> 00:31:11,620
we call it the run view

789
00:31:11,620 --> 00:31:12,620
and that's exactly what it means

790
00:31:12,620 --> 00:31:15,200
It's because the information described in here

791
00:31:15,200 --> 00:31:18,580
is only used at runtime

792
00:31:18,580 --> 00:31:19,400
if you see what I mean

793
00:31:19,400 --> 00:31:21,200
That's why it's called the run view

794
00:31:21,200 --> 00:31:25,620
So, our entire ELF file

795
00:31:25,620 --> 00:31:27,920
consists of these major parts

796
00:31:27,920 --> 00:31:30,280
and they each have their own meaning

797
00:31:30,280 --> 00:31:33,880
Now, things like segments and sections,

798
00:31:33,880 --> 00:31:36,060
we'll run into them a lot later on

799
00:31:36,060 --> 00:31:38,360
In particular, I think when you run programs,

800
00:31:38,360 --> 00:31:41,660
you'll often run into the famous segment fault

801
00:31:41,660 --> 00:31:44,520
Now you know why it's called a segment fault

802
00:31:44,520 --> 00:31:46,100
instead of a section fault

803
00:31:46,100 --> 00:31:46,620
Why is that?

804
00:31:46,620 --> 00:31:48,160
Because when you get an error

805
00:31:48,160 --> 00:31:49,340
a failure in memory

806
00:31:49,340 --> 00:31:50,760
he understands what I mean

807
00:31:50,760 --> 00:31:51,680
So this is

808
00:31:51,680 --> 00:31:53,580
All of these things

809
00:31:53,580 --> 00:31:56,700
come from these basic concepts

810
00:31:56,700 --> 00:31:58,840
So

811
00:31:58,840 --> 00:32:01,580
this is the

812
00:32:01,580 --> 00:32:06,240
basic format of ELF

813
00:32:06,240 --> 00:32:08,480
and it's a key focus of our study

814
00:32:08,480 --> 00:32:11,200
As for more detailed content

815
00:32:11,200 --> 00:32:13,720
you can probably go and check some reference books

816
00:32:13,720 --> 00:32:17,990
Now these ELF file formats

817
00:32:17,990 --> 00:32:19,230
when it comes to these files,

818
00:32:19,230 --> 00:32:20,930
if you asked us to manually

819
00:32:20,930 --> 00:32:22,250
analyze the contents ourselves

820
00:32:22,250 --> 00:32:24,150
it would be extremely tedious

821
00:32:24,150 --> 00:32:27,750
So GNU also provides

822
00:32:27,750 --> 00:32:30,470
a matching set of related tool programs.

823
00:32:30,470 --> 00:32:32,050
And these tool programs

824
00:32:32,050 --> 00:32:34,550
are all bundled together in one toolkit

825
00:32:34,550 --> 00:32:35,590
called Binutils.

826
00:32:35,590 --> 00:32:36,810
It is actually

827
00:32:36,810 --> 00:32:39,410
bin, b-i-n-u-t-i-l-s,

828
00:32:39,410 --> 00:32:40,650
which is actually an abbreviation of two words.

829
00:32:40,650 --> 00:32:42,330
So "bin" stands for binary,

830
00:32:42,330 --> 00:32:44,350
as we know, that means binary files,

831
00:32:44,350 --> 00:32:44,750
right?

832
00:32:44,750 --> 00:32:46,910
U-t-i-l-s stands for utilities,

833
00:32:46,910 --> 00:32:47,870
meaning tools.

834
00:32:47,870 --> 00:32:50,070
So the full name of this software

835
00:32:50,070 --> 00:32:51,410
is actually Binary Utilities,

836
00:32:51,410 --> 00:32:53,770
that is, binary tool programs.

837
00:32:53,770 --> 00:32:55,950
Inside here is really a software toolkit,

838
00:32:55,950 --> 00:32:58,110
which contains a lot of small programs.

839
00:32:58,110 --> 00:33:00,570
Each of these small programs has its own specific purpose,

840
00:33:00,570 --> 00:33:03,130
and we will touch on them later.

841
00:33:03,130 --> 00:33:04,950
So let me also introduce to you here

842
00:33:04,950 --> 00:33:07,130
some of the commonly used ones

843
00:33:07,130 --> 00:33:07,930
For example, AR,

844
00:33:07,930 --> 00:33:10,710
which we also often use, is called TAR,

845
00:33:10,710 --> 00:33:12,310
which is actually pretty much the same as AR.

846
00:33:12,310 --> 00:33:14,670
Then archiving is just packaging,

847
00:33:14,670 --> 00:33:16,290
it's packaging, right?

848
00:33:16,290 --> 00:33:18,210
For example, when we want to generate a static library,

